      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since we are using C++23, we can significantly clean up `parseHex` by leveraging `\u003cspanstream\u003e`. \n\nCurrently, the implementation creates a temporary `std::string` allocation just to satisfy the null-terminated `const char*` requirement of the C-style `strtol`, which also forces us to do manual `errno` and pointer-state checking.\n\nBy using `std::ispanstream`, we can bind directly to the memory of the `std::string_view` (zero allocations!) and use familiar, type-safe stream extraction. \n\n**Suggested replacement:**\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003cspanstream\u003e\n\n// ...\n\n\tstd::optional\u003cuint16_t\u003e parseHex(std::string_view strv)\n\t{\n\t\tif (strv.empty()) {\n\t\t\treturn std::nullopt;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tuint16_t val;\n\t\tstd::ispanstream stream(strv);\n\n\t\t// Extract as hex, and ensure we consumed the entire view\n\t\tif (stream \u003e\u003e std::hex \u003e\u003e val \u0026\u0026 stream.eof()) {\n\t\t\treturn val;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\treturn std::nullopt;\n\t}\n```\n\nThis makes the function much shorter, safer, and avoids the heap allocation on every CLI argument parse.",
